I&#x27;m using the &lt;template&gt; tag heavily in Timelinize[0], which has a fairly sophisticated UI that I&#x27;m writing in vanilla JS -- not even jQuery. I use a few libraries (for mapping, datetime, and Bootstrap+Tabler for some nice styles), but that&#x27;s it.<p>I wrote some boilerplate JS, yes, but I have complete control over my frontend, and I understand how it works, and I don&#x27;t need to compile it.<p>Anyway, it works well so far! The &lt;template&gt; tag is a great way to lay out components and then fill them in with simple JS functions.<p>One nuance is documented in my code comments:<p><pre><code>    &#x2F;&#x2F; Ohhhhhh wow, we need to use firstElementChild when cloning the content of a template tag (!!!!):
    &#x2F;&#x2F; https:&#x2F;&#x2F;developer.mozilla.org&#x2F;en-US&#x2F;docs&#x2F;Web&#x2F;HTML&#x2F;Reference&#x2F;Elements&#x2F;template#avoiding_documentfragment_pitfalls
    &#x2F;&#x2F; I spent way too long on this.
    const elem = $(tplSelector);
    if (!elem) return;
    return elem.content.firstElementChild.cloneNode(true);
</code></pre>
Once I figured that out, it&#x27;s been smooth sailing and very powerful.<p>Oh, but I haven&#x27;t cleaned up my code at all yet because I was just experimenting&#x2F;hustling, so it&#x27;s a bit spaghetti :) If you go looking, don&#x27;t mind the mess.<p>---<p>[0]: <a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;timelinize&#x2F;timelinize" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;timelinize&#x2F;timelinize</a> - a self-hosted application for bringing together all your data on your own computer and organizing it onto a single timeline: social media accounts, photo libraries, text messages, GPS tracks, browsing history, and more. Almost ready for its debut...

